  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/528bf1bc9d6177f031b8b5504641903a11dfc2c2576e4759bf4cb7c278d0585c.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=0$ is a sink\n- $y=3$ is a source\n- $y=3$ is a node",
  "worked_solution": "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{\u2032}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=0$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points upward. In other words, both arrows point *toward* the point $y=0.$ This means that $y=0$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=3$ points upward, and the arrow just below it points downward. In other words, both arrows point *away* from $y=3.$ This means that $y=3$ is a source.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
